00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:23.220
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:24.928
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
talked about storage.

00:05:24.928 --> 00:05:28.160
And we basically covered the
whole spectrum of storage

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.120
and discussed about
what product to use,

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:32.800
when to use one or the other.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.230
And I think that the most
interesting one, or at least

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:39.890
one of the most interesting
products, because it basically

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:41.990
powers Google, is Bigtable.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.340
And it turns out that you
are pretty much the expert

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.530
in the team about Bigtable.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:48.620
So could you tell
us a little bit

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.860
about what is Bigtable from
like, I think it's called,

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.050
10,000 feet view?

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:53.800
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:54.610
That 10,000 feet
sounds reasonable?

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:55.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
14,000 feet view?

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:56.280
I don't know.

00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.530
But from very high level.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:58.370
IAN LEWIS: Right.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:00.960
So there's quite a
difference, like there's

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.385
5,000 versus 10,000 feet,

00:06:02.385 --> 00:06:03.468
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah.

00:06:03.468 --> 00:06:04.260
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:09.120
IAN LEWIS: So bigtable is like
basically the NoSQL database.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.850
It was the kind of
the original one THAT

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.370
started the whole NoSQL idea.

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:16.240
And so Google's been using
it for quite a long time

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.830
just as the kind
of go-to database

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:23.010
for its very large applications,
like Gmail and Search and Maps.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:26.100
And it was originally published
kind of as a white paper.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.850
And then the ideas around
the architecture of Bigtable

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:31.920
were incorporated
into open source

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:37.510
projects, like HBase and
Cassandra and things like that.

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:39.530
And so from a technical
point of view,

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:42.670
it's actually a key
value data store.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:47.260
But it allows you to
have many values per key.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:50.270
And then it allows you
to do range scans by key.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:54.900
So in kind of relational
database parlance,

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.960
it's you have a
primary index, but you

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.750
don't have a secondary index.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:02.930
So you have what's
basically called columns,

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:05.850
but those columns, you
can't actually index those,

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.682
so you can't do queries on
them and that sort of thing.

00:07:08.682 --> 00:07:10.890
MARK MANDEL: So when you
say you can do a range scan,

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.500
what does that
kind of look like?

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:17.290
IAN LEWIS: So you can kind think
of the keys in the Bigtable

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:22.130
as being sorted by the00
lexicographically, basically.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:24.750
And so they're basically
sorted, and you can pick a key

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:28.170
and say, OK, from this key,
I want to do a range scan of,

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.030
and then just basically continue
to iterate over those keys.

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:36.410
So you can say, so from
key A get 100 keys.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:40.408
And then in that way, you can do
things like paging or whatnot.

00:07:40.408 --> 00:07:41.324
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:41.324 --> 00:07:42.158
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:07:42.158 --> 00:07:43.532
So you can kind
of get whatever's

00:07:43.532 --> 00:07:44.580
in that ordered range--

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:45.710
IAN LEWIS: Right, exactly.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:46.640
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:47.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Very interesting.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:49.090
So this is actually
sounds sort a little bit--

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.680
the story about Bigtable sounds
a little bit like MapReduce

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.540
where basically Google writes
a paper, and then at the end,

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.130
someone else implements
it, not Google.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:02.590
So I'm assuming HBase
is the open source

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:03.947
implementation of Bigtable?

00:08:03.947 --> 00:08:05.030
IAN LEWIS: Right, exactly.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:10.880
And so HBase was an
open source project

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:15.920
that was created based on
the Bigtable white paper.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.100
But now that
Bigtable is something

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.100
that we are releasing as
part of Cloud Platform,

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.990
so that other
developers can use it,

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:28.560
we decided to actually make it
available via the HBase API.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.030
So you can use the
exact same API as HBase

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:33.850
in order to access
Bigtable, which

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.654
is pretty powerful because
Bigtable is completely fully

00:08:36.654 --> 00:08:42.022
managed, and it makes it really
easy to set up and get running.

00:08:42.022 --> 00:08:43.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is very cool.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.760
I really like the idea
Google created Bigtable, not

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:49.800
open sourcing it, and
then making it available

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:53.677
and having the API
to something that was

00:08:53.677 --> 00:08:54.885
created from the [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:54.885 --> 00:08:55.594
IAN LEWIS: Right.

00:08:55.594 --> 00:08:57.801
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I really
like open source community.

00:08:57.801 --> 00:08:58.830
[INAUDIBLE] [? funny. ?]

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:00.400
IAN LEWIS: Well, I
think that Google

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.620
is kind of modifying their
stance a little bit on that.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.227
So like things like
TensorFlow and Kubernetes

00:09:06.227 --> 00:09:07.810
are things that are
kind of coming out

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:11.800
that where Google's actually
providing or creating

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:15.680
an open source implementation
that folks can kind of build

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:19.660
on, rather than just releasing
a white paper from the White

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:20.420
Tower, you know?

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:21.336
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:09:21.336 --> 00:09:24.040
Yeah, no, I really like the
idea of now more like the paper

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:25.130
comes with code.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:25.990
That's very nice.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:26.656
IAN LEWIS: Ight.

00:09:26.656 --> 00:09:27.572
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:27.572 --> 00:09:31.130
So when I joined Google, I
joined as a software engineer.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:32.620
And it's true what
you're saying.

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:35.970
Basically, everyone uses
Bigtable for everything.

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:39.976
But if you are not Google,
why could you use Bigtable?

00:09:39.976 --> 00:09:41.600
IAN LEWIS: So there's
a few reasons why

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.000
you might want to use Bigtable.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:47.350
So one is that
Bigtable's very fast.

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:50.250
You can make queries
on Bigtable or gets

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.620
and puts and things like
that, both reads and writes,

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.910
in about less than
tens of milliseconds.

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:59.200
So it's a very fast database.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:01.990
At the same time, it's
also a database that

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:03.460
will scale to very large size.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:07.620
So I mentioned that it's very
limited in what it can actually

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:13.000
do, like you only have
a primary key index.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.380
But that those
limits actually allow

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:19.740
you to scale to very, very
large sizes, so things like,

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.778
basically many
petabytes in size.

00:10:22.778 --> 00:10:24.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:26.350
IAN LEWIS: But there
might be some use cases

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:29.980
where you would
want that, and that

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:32.060
would be like things like
time-series databases,

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:33.177
analytics.

00:10:33.177 --> 00:10:35.010
Those are the ones that
are very easy to get

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:37.054
started using Bigtable.

00:10:37.054 --> 00:10:37.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:40.880
You said that
Bigtable's really fast.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:43.070
And you actually gave
the order of magnitude

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:44.490
about ten milliseconds.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:47.470
For our listeners, what is the
order of magnitude of others,

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:51.130
like, for instance, like
Datastore or, for instance,

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:52.710
on MySQL databases?

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:54.960
IAN LEWIS: So MySQL
will be pretty

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:59.847
fast for things like
basically getting by key

00:10:59.847 --> 00:11:00.680
or selecting by key.

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:03.970
But for things like
very complex queries,

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:05.760
it could take
orders of magnitude

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:09.970
longer, like many, many seconds
or many tens of seconds.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:12.960
And things like
Datastore generally

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:18.180
come back in the tens of
milliseconds or hundreds

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.940
of milliseconds,
depending on the queries

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:24.160
that you're actually doing, So
Bigtable is quite a bit faster,

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:28.340
and so, in that sense, it's
very good for kind of more

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:30.010
low latency applications.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:31.050
MARK MANDEL: And I'm
guessing you probably

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:32.758
don't have similar
issues, like you would

00:11:32.758 --> 00:11:35.134
with, say, MySQL, where once
a table gets really large,

00:11:35.134 --> 00:11:37.300
you start having like
slowdowns and stuff like that.

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:38.383
IAN LEWIS: Right, exactly.

00:11:38.383 --> 00:11:43.130
So I mentioned that MySQL will
be fast for keys, selecting

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:47.270
just a key, but it will
actually get much slower

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:51.900
when it's actually overloaded
or gets very large.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:53.400
MARK MANDEL: So
we're basically sort

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:57.430
of thinking so Bigtable
probably exists for those really

00:11:57.430 --> 00:11:58.670
big data applications.

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:00.275
IAN LEWIS: Right, exactly.

00:12:00.275 --> 00:12:01.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
There's actually

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.630
one of my favorite things
that you can do with Bigtable,

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:11.970
and which we used for a
funny demo for Pi Day.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:13.740
It's the fact that
you don't need

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.230
to know the whole key,
that you can just say,

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.592
give me all the keys that
starts with this prefix.

00:12:18.592 --> 00:12:19.300
IAN LEWIS: Right.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Could you tell--

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:21.470
MARK MANDEL: Oh, is
that how that works?

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It is
pretty amazing, yeah.

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:23.970
MARK MANDEL: Oh, OK.

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:24.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:26.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
you can be like, oh,

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:30.320
I'm actually just storing
keys of like 10 words.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.270
But if you give me
only five, I will still

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:33.890
find it because I
can say, give me

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:36.810
all the keys that start
with those five words--

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:37.783
five letters, sorry.

00:12:37.783 --> 00:12:38.570
IAN LEWIS: Right.

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:40.153
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
is pretty amazing.

00:12:40.153 --> 00:12:43.430
So I was able to
make it for my case,

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:46.350
but that's a very
specific and weird thing

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:48.490
to do to search stuff on pi.

00:12:48.490 --> 00:12:51.270
But have you ever seen people
doing these kind of things

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.120
in production, and what for?

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:58.190
IAN LEWIS: So, yeah, that's
a great thing to mention.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:01.020
So Bigtable allows you to
do several different types

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:04.840
of what they call filters, and
one of those is a prefix scan.

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:10.230
And so you can select
all of the keys that

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:12.200
have a particular prefix.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:14.180
And so you might
use that for things

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:16.730
like indexing, for instance.

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:19.340
Like in your case, you actually
indexed numbers of pi, right?

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:19.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:21.610
IAN LEWIS: And then you can
go and search those later.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:23.235
Well, you can do the
same sort of thing

00:13:23.235 --> 00:13:27.140
with text or with
just regular old keys.

00:13:27.140 --> 00:13:30.080
So this is actually
what Datastore

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:34.320
uses to do its secondary indexes
for when you actually save

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:36.852
entities and things like that.

00:13:36.852 --> 00:13:37.810
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:13:37.810 --> 00:13:40.170
So you could use like common
prefixes to group things

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:41.560
together and things like that?

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:42.185
IAN LEWIS: Yes.

00:13:42.185 --> 00:13:42.754
Yes, exactly.

00:13:42.754 --> 00:13:43.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:47.210
So then I think
that everybody then

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:50.040
is convinced that actually
Datastore is amazing.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:52.060
Ah, Datastore-- sorry.

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:55.050
Bigtable's amazing, and
everybody should be using it.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.250
But that's not the
case, even at Google.

00:13:57.250 --> 00:14:00.080
So why could someone
not use Bigtable?

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:01.840
IAN LEWIS: Yeah, so
there's a few reasons,

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:05.840
and these are the kind of
reasons why Datastore exists.

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:08.870
So Bigtable doesn't actually
support transactions.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:13.440
So it actually will
support allowing

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:18.710
you to write a single
row, and that row

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:21.330
will be written or not written.

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:24.090
So in that sense, you can
basically do transactions

00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:26.730
on a single set of
columns or a single row,

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:29.000
but you can't actually
do that across rows.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.300
So if you want to save
multiple rows at the same time,

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:34.050
you need some other kind of
transaction or locking-kind-of

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:35.890
mechanism.

00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:38.370
It's also not the
greatest database

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:40.180
for things like
high availability.

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:44.280
So if you have a
particular key, that key

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:48.050
may become unavailable
kind of sporadically,

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:52.130
depending on how that
database is being split up

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:54.530
and moved around
in the back end.

00:14:54.530 --> 00:15:00.930
And so if you need your database
to be like completely highly

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:05.790
available and for the keys
to be there all of the time,

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:10.690
then Bigtable might not
be exactly what you want.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:15.150
You also have the issue
of it being local.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:17.850
So it's local to
a specific region.

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:23.060
And so you can do replication to
other regions in the background

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:24.670
or asynchronously.

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:28.680
But if you need your database
to be kind of globally

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:30.300
available or
something like that,

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:32.870
then Bigtable might
not be something

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:36.390
that you'll want to have.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:39.396
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:15:39.396 --> 00:15:40.354
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:15:40.354 --> 00:15:41.970
What about the cost?

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:45.670
Because how do you
pay for a Bigtable?

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:48.292
Is it like Datastore, where
you pay per operation, or?

00:15:48.292 --> 00:15:49.000
IAN LEWIS: Right.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.230
So Bigtable's actually a
little bit more predictable

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:53.130
than Datastore.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:56.160
So Bigtable, actually,
you pay for the cost,

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:59.870
and then you pay
for the nodes that

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:03.780
are set up to allow
you to make queries

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:05.430
on the database and
that sort of thing.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:11.390
And so what you pay for is like
per-hour per-node as well as

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:15.120
the storage in per gigabyte.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:18.190
I don't remember exactly
what it is right now,

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:23.400
but the minimum cost for
the nodes is about $1,400

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:27.270
per month plus storage.

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:28.460
So you pay in that sense.

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:33.630
And so I mentioned nodes,
and that's something

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:36.390
that I think that I
should probably mention

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:41.280
is that there are nodes
that you set up in Bigtable,

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:42.260
and you pay per node.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.150
But these nodes
basically provide you

00:16:44.150 --> 00:16:46.330
throughput into the database.

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:49.990
So you basically get
about 10,000 queries

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:51.190
per second per node.

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:58.060
And so if you need
more than say 30,000,

00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:00.880
which is the minimum--
three nodes is the minimum.

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:05.430
If you need more than 30,000,
then you'll set up more nodes

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:08.410
and basically allow you to
do more throughput there.

00:17:08.410 --> 00:17:11.060
MARK MANDEL: So really, as your
throughput requirements grow,

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:12.640
the more nodes
you're going to need.

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:15.419
Therefore your cost increases,
and you can trade that off.

00:17:15.419 --> 00:17:15.859
IAN LEWIS: Right, exactly.

00:17:15.859 --> 00:17:17.190
But what's interesting
about Bigtable

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:18.859
is that the nodes actually
don't store the data.

00:17:18.859 --> 00:17:20.109
The data is stored separately.

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:22.329
And so you can kind of
increase and decrease

00:17:22.329 --> 00:17:27.750
the number of nodes like pretty
separately from how much data

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:28.250
you store.

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:32.050
So say you have a lot of
data, but you don't actually

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:33.460
need that many QPS.

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:35.360
You can actually just
have it three nodes.

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:38.330
And your storage can
be astronomically big,

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:42.382
but you're just using
three nodes to access it.

00:17:42.382 --> 00:17:44.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And what
is that, that there's

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:45.785
a minimum of three nodes?

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.250
IAN LEWIS: So the
minimum of three nodes

00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:53.450
is basically there to allow
a little bit of redundancy

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:55.890
for the nodes, because
those can be kind of moved

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:59.140
around a little bit also.

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:03.900
But basically, when you
create a Bigtable cluster,

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:08.790
essentially you have a minimum
of three nodes to set up.

00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:12.450
And so that's
basically kind of a way

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:17.456
of allowing kind of a minimal
level of service for Bigtable.

00:18:17.456 --> 00:18:18.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:23.130
So something that I used for
the pi finder for that demo

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:26.660
was actually that idea of being
able to add nodes and remove

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:27.390
them.

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:29.120
And I was lucky
enough that I got

00:18:29.120 --> 00:18:32.080
to work with the Bigtable team.

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:36.500
So I ran a data flow process,
where I was processing

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:38.000
a huge amount of data.

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:39.900
I mean, pi big.

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:42.430
And on top of that, I
was storing everything

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.020
to Bigtable at the same time.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:50.325
So I was able to run a Bigtable
cluster with 500 nodes.

00:18:50.325 --> 00:18:51.660
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:53.260
IAN LEWIS: Quite awesome

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:55.220
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
was incredibly amazing.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:58.390
I had like, I think it
was, like over 1,000 nodes

00:18:58.390 --> 00:19:02.030
in data flow trying to
basically write as much as I

00:19:02.030 --> 00:19:04.362
could directly to Bigtable.

00:19:04.362 --> 00:19:06.570
And Bigtable didn't even
shake. [? It was ?] amazing.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:08.820
MARK MANDEL: What sort of
throughput were you getting?

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:09.370
Do you know?

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:11.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Actually, I
didn't look all the time at it.

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:14.450
But I know that it
passed really, really

00:19:14.450 --> 00:19:18.260
fast one million requests per
second, really, really easily.

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:19.582
And I was like, oh, wow.

00:19:19.582 --> 00:19:20.290
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:19:20.290 --> 00:19:21.206
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:19:21.206 --> 00:19:23.570
And then the good thing
is that once it was done

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:26.240
and it had all pi
stored in Bigtable,

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.020
I took it down to three.

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:29.931
And everything went
really fast, which

00:19:29.931 --> 00:19:32.430
is very nice, because then it's
like oh, yeah, I'm done now.

00:19:32.430 --> 00:19:33.880
I have my process already done.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:35.255
Like if you're
running map reduce

00:19:35.255 --> 00:19:37.670
or whatever, once the
map reduce is over,

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:40.250
if you need to read from it,
you don't need those nodes,

00:19:40.250 --> 00:19:41.820
like all the nodes you
had at the beginning.

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:42.790
And that's really cool.

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:44.590
IAN LEWIS: Yeah.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:46.650
That's one benefit of me
being on the same team

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:49.590
is I've seen the same exact
things that you guys have.

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:52.630
But, yeah, I think
the graph was actually

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:54.450
about close to two million.

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:55.866
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Two million, wow.

00:19:55.866 --> 00:19:57.630
IAN LEWIS: Which is
pretty impressive.

00:19:57.630 --> 00:19:58.610
MARK MANDEL: Well, what's
one and two million?

00:19:58.610 --> 00:20:00.026
I mean, like it's
the same number.

00:20:00.026 --> 00:20:01.870
IAN LEWIS: Yeah, you
can also say, though,

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:07.170
that we do have customers that
do quite a bit more than that.

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:10.620
So it's a database that scales.

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:12.670
Internally, we
also have customers

00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:16.850
that do very, very large
amounts of queries per second.

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:22.130
And I also mentioned that it's
like 10 milliseconds per query.

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:24.520
But that basically
doesn't change no matter

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:27.450
the size of the data
as well as that's

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:30.720
also like a P99 type of thing.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:34.910
So basically, 99% of the
queries will be faster

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:36.130
than 10 milliseconds.

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:38.560
So it's very, very
consistent in terms

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:41.350
of how fast the
queries are, which

00:20:41.350 --> 00:20:43.854
is very advantageous
for a lot of use cases.

00:20:43.854 --> 00:20:44.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:44.770 --> 00:20:45.270
OK.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:48.100
So let's imagine that I
wanted to use Bigtable,

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:50.950
but I'm actually using
something different,

00:20:50.950 --> 00:20:53.120
like for instance,
I'm using Datastore.

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:56.327
How big of a change could
that be for the developer?

00:20:56.327 --> 00:20:58.410
IAN LEWIS: Yeah, that could
be quite a big change.

00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:01.780
It depends on what you're
doing with Datastore.

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:06.670
If you're doing things that are
very kind of low level, where

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:09.480
you're only doing
gets and puts by key,

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:12.420
then that can translate
pretty easily to Bigtable.

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:15.994
And so I think it wouldn't
be too hard to migrate.

00:21:15.994 --> 00:21:18.160
But other things, where
you're doing lots of queries

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:20.612
and you're using
lots of indexes,

00:21:20.612 --> 00:21:23.070
those types of situations would
be pretty difficult to move

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:24.140
over, I think.

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:26.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And what
about if I was already using

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:28.789
HBase and I wanted
to use Bigtable?

00:21:28.789 --> 00:21:30.330
IAN LEWIS: So that's
a good question.

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:35.280
So the Bigtable does
support the HBase API.

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:40.260
There's a few of edge cases,
that it doesn't support.

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:42.225
Those are mostly around
managing the HBase,

00:21:42.225 --> 00:21:45.640
so like doing compactions
and adding nodes and things

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:46.159
like that.

00:21:46.159 --> 00:21:47.700
And Bigtable's kind
of fully managed,

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:50.380
so you don't really have
to deal with any of that.

00:21:50.380 --> 00:21:54.310
But there are a few like
kind of small edge cases

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:55.310
that it doesn't support.

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:58.360
But, for the most
part, it supports

00:21:58.360 --> 00:22:00.370
the majority of the HBase API.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:02.600
And so it's very,
very easy to move

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:06.880
from an HBase installation or an
HBase cluster over to Bigtable.

00:22:06.880 --> 00:22:09.910
Probably the hardest thing
will be moving the data over.

00:22:09.910 --> 00:22:11.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's fair enough.

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:13.314
MARK MANDEL: Well,
before we wrap up here,

00:22:13.314 --> 00:22:15.480
is there anything about
Bigtable you want to mention

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:17.655
or anything else that we
may have managed to miss?

00:22:17.655 --> 00:22:19.030
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Any cool project

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:21.940
that you would like to mention
that you've seen before,

00:22:21.940 --> 00:22:24.210
other than my thing
searching for pi.

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:26.250
I'm sure the people have
more interesting stuff

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:27.880
than searching pi.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:31.800
IAN LEWIS: So Google has made
quite a lot of cool projects,

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:33.880
and all of them pretty
much use Bigtable.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:36.980
But what I think is
interesting about Bigtable

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:39.240
is that it's used
kind of at Google

00:22:39.240 --> 00:22:41.790
as the basis for a lot of
other types of data stores.

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:45.540
And so things like Datastore
and Cloud Datastore

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:47.440
are kind of built
on top of Bigtable

00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:51.590
to allow like secondary
indexes and things like that.

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:54.200
So I think that
like how Bigtable

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:59.170
is used kind of as a low-level
storage is pretty interesting.

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:14.320
And I think that just the sheer
scale and the amount of work

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:19.000
that's gone into it at
Google is quite amazing.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:22.930
We've spent probably about a
year and millions of man hours

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:24.370
improving Bigtable.

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:26.270
And so it's very,
very optimized when

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:29.510
you compare it to things
like HBase and Cassandra.

00:23:29.510 --> 00:23:34.150
Cassandra can do things
like, it can write very fast,

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:35.900
but it can't read very fast.

00:23:35.900 --> 00:23:37.480
But Bigtable can
kind of do both.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.270
And so it's very,
very interesting

00:23:39.270 --> 00:23:41.470
that it can read really
fast and write really

00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:43.520
fast and get really big.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:48.220
So it's almost like
this magical thing

00:23:48.220 --> 00:23:52.670
that we've been able to improve
and build upon over the last 10

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:53.566
years or so.

00:23:53.566 --> 00:23:54.440
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:56.280
Well, Ian, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:57.830
Really appreciate
you taking the time.

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:58.890
IAN LEWIS: Yeah,
it's been great.

00:23:58.890 --> 00:23:59.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank you.

00:23:59.430 --> 00:24:00.190
IAN LEWIS: Thanks.

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:01.606
MARK MANDEL: Thanks
so much to Ian

00:24:01.606 --> 00:24:03.394
for joining us for
that interview.

00:24:03.394 --> 00:24:04.310
I learned a whole lot.

00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:05.900
I really didn't
actually know very much

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:06.816
at all about Bigtable.

00:24:06.816 --> 00:24:08.659
And now I have a
much better idea.

00:24:08.659 --> 00:24:10.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, no, Bigtable's

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:13.790
a very, very cool product,
and it is pretty amazing.

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:14.540
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:15.998
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, once you get

00:24:15.998 --> 00:24:18.570
started and you start
using it-- and have not

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.850
seen the point where it's like,
oh, yeah, it's not slow enough.

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:22.920
Nope, not there yet.

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:23.586
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:24:23.586 --> 00:24:24.380
No.

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:25.820
Excellent.

00:24:25.820 --> 00:24:27.780
But we do have a very
interesting Question

00:24:27.780 --> 00:24:29.700
of the Week.

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:32.960
The question kind of comes from
managing, say, a Google Cloud

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:34.019
Platform project.

00:24:34.019 --> 00:24:35.560
And within that
project, you probably

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:36.820
have a whole bunch
of different things.

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:38.861
Like maybe you have stuff
deployed to App Engine,

00:24:38.861 --> 00:24:41.320
stuff to push into
Cloud Storage, maybe

00:24:41.320 --> 00:24:43.060
stuff on Compute
Engine instances.

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:45.825
And you have someone new join
your team, and you want to say,

00:24:45.825 --> 00:24:48.890
oh, OK, I want them to be able
to deploy, say, Compute Engine

00:24:48.890 --> 00:24:51.120
instances, but I don't want
them to be able to read

00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:52.200
from, say, Cloud Storage.

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:53.550
Maybe you have like
private keys there

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:56.091
or something like that, that
are only limited to certain team

00:24:56.091 --> 00:24:57.190
members.

00:24:57.190 --> 00:24:59.190
Can you do that?

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:02.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Well,
no, until today, actually.

00:25:02.690 --> 00:25:04.660
MARK MANDEL: Oh, until today.

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:06.660
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Until
today-- until today right

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:12.395
now, Wednesday,
March 23rd-- 23rd.

00:25:12.395 --> 00:25:13.145
MARK MANDEL: 23rd.

00:25:13.145 --> 00:25:13.388
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:25:13.388 --> 00:25:15.910
23rd, that's it. (LAUGHING)
I'm really bad with dates.

00:25:15.910 --> 00:25:16.660
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:18.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so
actually today we announced

00:25:18.970 --> 00:25:23.810
this really cool thing,
which is Cloud I-A-M-- I-A-M,

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:25.790
or Cloud "I am"
or Cloud "ee-um,"

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:27.710
if you want to pronounce
it in a weird way.

00:25:27.710 --> 00:25:30.360
MARK MANDEL: So Cloud Identity
and Access Management.

00:25:30.360 --> 00:25:31.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If you
want to be correct, yes.

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:32.338
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:25:32.338 --> 00:25:32.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:38.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, and
basically, it allows you to,

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:42.100
basically, you talk
about what things

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:44.200
are people allowed to do.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:49.140
So far, we had three roles
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:51.860
We had the role of
honor, that was basically

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:55.770
allowed to do anything;
the role of editor, that

00:25:55.770 --> 00:25:57.199
was allowed to do some things

00:25:57.199 --> 00:25:58.240
MARK MANDEL: Most things.

00:25:58.240 --> 00:25:59.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Most
things, but not everything.

00:25:59.800 --> 00:25:59.960
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:02.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And then
the role of viewer, that was

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:04.305
allows us to view things
but not like create

00:26:04.305 --> 00:26:05.430
an instance of [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:06.210
MARK MANDEL: No.

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:06.850
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very limited.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:09.370
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so if I
wanted to grant someone access,

00:26:09.370 --> 00:26:11.710
they pretty much got
access to mostly all of it.

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:13.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, basically.

00:26:13.210 --> 00:26:17.670
So now you can actually
create way more roles,

00:26:17.670 --> 00:26:19.267
and we already did that.

00:26:19.267 --> 00:26:20.850
We created a bunch
of different roles.

00:26:20.850 --> 00:26:23.400
So for instance, you
can say, oh, actually

00:26:23.400 --> 00:26:28.230
someone is going to be a Compute
Engine instance administrator.

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:32.060
So that person will be
allowed to create instances,

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:34.100
to delete them if needed.

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:38.350
But they will not be able to
modify a Google Cloud Storage

00:26:38.350 --> 00:26:39.409
bucket for instance.

00:26:39.409 --> 00:26:41.950
MARK MANDEL: So you can really
tailor exactly what permission

00:26:41.950 --> 00:26:44.272
levels people have within
the system to exactly

00:26:44.272 --> 00:26:45.480
what your business needs are.

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:46.521
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Exactly.

00:26:46.521 --> 00:26:50.690
And that building on top of
the best practice of using

00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:52.786
multiple projects for
different environments,

00:26:52.786 --> 00:26:54.160
you could see
something where you

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:56.285
can have like [INAUDIBLE]
[? action ?] environment,

00:26:56.285 --> 00:26:58.720
like production product,
QA product, test product.

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:00.690
And you have different
roles for those people.

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:04.740
So someone would be able to do--
like everybody will be an honor

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:07.800
and be allowed to do everything
in the project for [? tests. ?]

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:11.830
But maybe in QA you cannot
do all of those things.

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:14.570
And maybe in production
you're able not to do anything

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:18.470
unless you are a DevOps
and then you are actually

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:21.650
allowed to dig down
instances and delete data

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:23.250
and do crazy things like that.

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:23.570
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:26.028
And I'm guessing yo probably
also like build tools as well.

00:27:26.028 --> 00:27:28.260
So like you can't physically
go in and modify stuff,

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:30.301
but if you go through the
administration console,

00:27:30.301 --> 00:27:33.070
like a custom-built thing or
like some DevOps-type scripting

00:27:33.070 --> 00:27:35.460
environment, then you're
like, OK, that's OK.

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:37.370
But then it sort of
mitigates how much

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:41.229
problems you can kind of
do by just kind of poking

00:27:41.229 --> 00:27:42.770
around and seeing
what sort of things

00:27:42.770 --> 00:27:45.790
happen when I put a spanner
in the electrical socket.

00:27:45.790 --> 00:27:47.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: (LAUGHING)
Yeah, you should not do that.

00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:48.090
MARK MANDEL: You should
probably not do that.

00:27:48.090 --> 00:27:48.380
Don't do that.

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:50.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Kids, don't do that.

00:27:50.100 --> 00:27:52.058
Yeah, and on top of that,
there's also the fact

00:27:52.058 --> 00:27:54.190
that when I say
identity, it kind of

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:56.860
sounds like it's only
for people but not only.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:28:00.400
You can have the same
thing for instances

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:03.730
or any kind of machines but
by using service accounts.

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:06.030
So you can basically
say, oh, this instance

00:28:06.030 --> 00:28:09.410
is allowed to read
from this bucket,

00:28:09.410 --> 00:28:10.890
or these instance is able to--

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:11.806
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:28:11.806 --> 00:28:16.836
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we used to
have those things as tokens.

00:28:16.836 --> 00:28:20.510
You kind of have the same
granularity of-- sorry.

00:28:20.510 --> 00:28:22.920
Not tokens-- scopes
for [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:26.860
You have this kind of same
idea for every single one

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:29.534
of the roles applied to every
single one of the resources.

00:28:29.534 --> 00:28:30.200
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:32.450
So not only can you really
nail it down by user,

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:34.530
but you can be like,
yeah, this part could only

00:28:34.530 --> 00:28:35.710
access this particular part.

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:37.410
This part can only access
this particular part.

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:38.980
And you can get much
more secure about it.

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:40.521
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, basically, you

00:28:40.521 --> 00:28:43.650
get all the enhanced security
that a company like Google

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:44.890
could need.

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:48.930
Until now, it could be really
weird to have something like

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:51.360
a big, big company,
where in a big,

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:54.230
big company you cannot trust
every single engineer to do

00:28:54.230 --> 00:28:57.580
every single thing, Now you
can actually write it down.

00:28:57.580 --> 00:28:58.530
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

